<h2>The question</h2>

<p>The operator wants a live session and the unattended runner to add work and handle it identically.
<a href="/backlog/3722-decision-are-the-interactive-session-and-the-runner-one-syst/">#3722</a> asks whether that
means one system (the session is one more caller of the same operations) or two systems over shared operations.
The full working, with every probe and citation, is in
<a href="/reports/2026-09-21-session-runner-one-system-grounding.md">the session report</a>.</p>

<h2>What prior art says</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Finding</th><th>Adoptable here</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Kubernetes controllers</td>
      <td>Level-based, not edge-based. Status is the latest observation, never a tracked event. The store adds
        compare-and-swap.</td>
      <td>The discipline: list the world, compute what is wrong, fix one thing. Not the store.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>bors, Homu, rust-lang/bors, GitHub merge queue</td>
      <td>The original bors was stateless and exited each run. The successors added a database and re-sync from
        GitHub every 15-30 minutes. Humans and bots use the same queue.</td>
      <td>One entry point for humans and bots. Derive what you can; keep a small store for the rest.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Temporal, GitHub Actions, Argo Events</td>
      <td>One declared workflow, several triggers. Trigger identity is data, not a code fork. An overlap policy
        covers two callers at once. Scheduled runs can be dropped.</td>
      <td>Same shape as one declared operation with many callers. Ticks must be level-triggered.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Kubernetes Job, ephemeral CI runners</td>
      <td>Run-to-completion makes "process alive" mean "work in progress". A finished process that never exits
        is the classic zombie; the fix was structural.</td>
      <td>Launch one-turn workers so they exit. Keep a lease TTL for a worker that hangs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>git notes, per-checkout files</td>
      <td>Sync is opt-in and merges are manual. Operator intent works best where every reader sees it with no
        sync step.</td>
      <td>Not a per-checkout file plus a sync.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>What the code says</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Guard bookkeeping is mostly derived already. The build guard has a restart-surviving floor read from
    <code>claude agents</code> (#3403). The fix and ci-heal caps read the PR's own re-arm comments (#2643,
    #2666).</li>
  <li>Seven stores (queue, pause marker, run records, completion records, infra-blocked, jury ledger, red-main
    freeze) resolve their root from the script location, so a hook or session in a lane clone sees different
    files than the runner. Run records are the fact the card proposes to derive from, so this defect sits under
    its own default.</li>
  <li>The three liveness models are one source. The lane lease pid is dormant (it records the short-lived
    <code>acquire</code> CLI, not the agent). The run record stamp is a cache of the listing. Every reader calls
    one function, <code>defaultListAgents</code>.</li>
  <li>The pause marker's own header says it stops all new dispatch, and the lane ceiling is a standing ceiling,
    but both are checked only in the planners; no dispatch operation reads them. So "a session's dispatch is
    supervised by construction" is contradicted: load reached 28 on 12 cores on 2026-09-19 from hand dispatch.</li>
  <li>Run-to-completion is not a launch flag. The spawn is a blocking call with a 60-second kill, the
    lease reaper skips any session whose <code>kind</code> is not <code>background</code> (so a live
    <code>-p</code> worker's lane would be released), and the permission profile under <code>-p</code> is unprobed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Probes of claude -p (CLI 2.1.278, 2026-09-21)</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Exits when its turn ends?</td><td>Yes. Exit code 0, 4 seconds for a one-word reply.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Listed while running?</td><td>Yes: <code>kind: interactive</code>, <code>status: busy</code>, with a
      <code>pid</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Takes <code>-n &lt;slug&gt;</code>?</td><td>Yes. The row carried the name.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Leaves the listing on exit?</td><td>Yes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Resumable after exit?</td><td>Yes. <code>claude -p --resume &lt;id&gt;</code> returned the same session
      id and remembered the earlier turn.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Not probed</td><td>Worker permission profile under <code>-p</code>; background subagents holding a
      <code>-p</code> worker open (documented ceiling: 10 minutes); a hung worker.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>

<p>One loop, expressed as declared operations, with every actor a caller (the runner is a scheduler over them).
Every resource gate (pause, capacity, load, lane, in-flight) binds every caller and is enforced inside the
operation, which today it is not; the human/unattended difference lives only in how a refusal is delivered.
Move the seven shared sidecars to one machine-level home until the shared store (#2742) lands. Keep workers
resident and reap them on the completion record plus a deadline (#3721); run-to-completion is the better model
in principle but is gated on three preconditions the skeptic found: a supervisor shape (#3627 is open), every
consumer that filters on <code>kind: background</code>, and the worker permission profile under <code>-p</code>. The
four forks and their skeptic verdicts are in
<a href="/backlog/3722-decision-are-the-interactive-session-and-the-runner-one-syst/">#3722</a>.</p>

